Enumerate and program the virtualized cursor plane

virtio-gpu, vmwgfx and qxl set DRIVER_CURSOR_HOTSPOT, and the kernel then hides
the cursor plane from any atomic client that has not set
DRM_CLIENT_CAP_CURSOR_PLANE_HOTSPOT: drm_mode_getplane_res() skips cursor planes
outright for such clients (drm_plane.c), because a client that positions the
plane by its top-left corner would put the guest's pointer in the wrong place.

aquamarine only sets UNIVERSAL_PLANES and ATOMIC, so on those drivers it sees no
cursor plane at all, never advertises a pointer capability, and the compositor
silently falls back to a software cursor -- which on a virtual GPU costs a full
scanout flush per pointer move.

Set the cap once atomic is confirmed, and program HOTSPOT_X/Y alongside CRTC_X/Y
on the cursor plane. The existing CRTC_X/Y math (cursorPos - cursorHotspot) is
unchanged and stays correct for real hardware; the hotspot properties are what
let a virtualized driver recover the pointer position as CRTC_X/Y + HOTSPOT_X/Y.
The properties are already in the plane property table but were never written.
Both the shape and the move path set them, so a pointer move alone carries the
hotspot too. The legacy path already passes the hotspot via drmModeSetCursor2
and is untouched. Older kernels reject the cap; that is logged and ignored.

Equivalent to KWin's 7fdf0fb792 and bd2728fac1.
